  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The paper relies on illustrative cases rather than a systematic empirical dataset or comparative urban analysis.",
      "what": "This paper introduces the concept of the 'patronizing city' and establishes an analytical framework that connects urban governance, institutional practices, and the design of built environments to adult-centered planning. The framework is illustrated through qualitative case studies to demonstrate how adult-centrism systematically excludes young people from political, social, and spatial urban domains.",
      "why": "It provides a new conceptual vocabulary and structural framework to analyze how urban planning practices actively marginalize youth, shifting the conversation on urban sustainability to include age-based spatial justice."
